variables into the 2^{r-1} exact r-clauses that exclude all assignments of the
wrong parity.  Before applying that paper-central identity, the generator
chooses n=2h bits in opposite hidden pairs and a random directed h-cycle p.
It defines A over GF(2) by
  (Ax)_i     = x_i xor x_{p(i)} xor x_{h+p(i)},
  (Ax)_{h+i} = x_{h+i} xor x_{p(i)} xor x_{h+p(i)}.
Writing A=I+N, the two block rows of N are equal and N^2=0, hence A^2=I.
The sampled assignment x is evaluated to obtain r=Ax; each row is converted
to four exact 3-clauses.  Balanced decoy pairs are sampled around the same
assignment.  Their two forbidden tuples are complements, so literal signs
remain balanced.  The known x is carried through a random variable relabelling.
No SAT solver, Gaussian elimination, or certificate search is used to build it.

Reference algorithm and compact route. Group clauses by unsigned variable
triple; groups containing the four wrong-parity tuples are 3-XOR rows.  Exact
GF(2) Gaussian elimination is O(m log m+n^3), succeeds on every instance, and
is reported separately as Track B requires.  The compact route recognizes
A^2=I and applies the recovered row operator once more, costing two binary
XORs per output bit after the invariant has been found.

Attacks. Complete parity blocks and complementary decoy pairs tie every
variable's positive and negative literal counts, defeating signed-occurrence
outliers. Public-order alternation is destroyed by relabelling. Greedy clause
repair is checked with a 2n-step budget, uniform random restart uses the exact
n-bit certificate language, and the in-context XOR-RHS vote recognizes blocks
but ignores their coupling. All are rerun across eight shipping seeds. The
successful elimination algorithm is disclosed under reference_algorithm,
never misreported as a failed attack.
""".strip()


