../../results/2603.30009/codex_run.log:5881:../2503.13722/REJECTED.md:41:The costs were measured for the exposed-map version. Relabelling the 35 blocks of size 17 writes 595 point labels, constructing the inverse permutation writes 35 entries, and constructing the conjugated involution writes 35 entries: 665 coarse table operations and 630 answer/instance atoms. A CPython microbenchmark of those operations took 0.188621 seconds for 20,000 repetitions, or 9.43 microseconds per instance. The compact route has the same 665-operation construction (or the same 35 conjugation evaluations if the already-relabeled incidence matrix is not part of the requested answer), so the mechanical/compact gap is 1:1. Under the natural block-list certificate, the 630 atomic outputs also exceed G9(c)'s 256-element cap.
../../results/2603.30009/codex_run.log:5882:../2503.13722/REJECTED.md:43:For comparison, the paper's classification route has a very large mechanical cost—at least 18,894 orbit matrices and 11,670,332 per-action design representatives—but there is no compact route at all. Asking for the final number 11,642,495 makes the answer a classification-table lookup, while asking the solver to establish completeness makes the checker rerun research-level enumeration and isomorphism rejection.
